{
 "war": "imjin-war",
 "start": "1592-05-22",
 "end": "1598-12-22",
 "note": "The shaded area is the ground the Japanese armies held, reconstructed from campaign maps and battle accounts; their hold away from the main roads and castles was often loose, and guerrillas operated inside it. Naval battles are marked at sea.",
 "chapters": [
  {
   "id": "busan-to-hanseong",
   "title": "From Busan to Hanseong",
   "start": "1592-05-22",
   "end": "1592-06-12",
   "bbox": [
    125.8,
    34.6,
    130.0,
    38.2
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "The First Division lands at Busan (23 May 1592)",
    "Konishi's march from Busan to Hanseong (May–June 1592)",
    "Katō's march by way of Gyeongju (May–June 1592)"
   ],
   "text": "In May 1592 Toyotomi Hideyoshi, who had just unified Japan, sent about 158,000 men to Korea as the first step towards conquering China. Konishi Yukinaga's division landed at Busan on 23 May and stormed Busanjin and Dongnae. Three Japanese columns raced north, crushed General Yi Il at Sangju and Sin Rip's army at Chungju, and on 10 June Konishi entered the capital Hanseong, abandoned by King Seonjo, less than three weeks after landing."
  },
  {
   "id": "the-north",
   "title": "Pyongyang and the far north",
   "start": "1592-06-12",
   "end": "1592-09-05",
   "bbox": [
    124.0,
    36.8,
    130.8,
    43.0
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Konishi advances to Pyongyang (June–July 1592)",
    "Katō marches into Hamgyong (July–August 1592)",
    "Zu Chengxun's Ming force attacks Pyongyang (August 1592)"
   ],
   "text": "From Hanseong the Japanese pushed north. After destroying a Korean army at the Imjin river in early July, Konishi took Pyongyang on 24 July, and King Seonjo fled to Uiju on the Chinese border. Katō Kiyomasa marched up the east coast through Hamgyong and on 30 August reached Hoeryong near the Tumen, where two Korean princes were handed over to him. A first, small Ming army was cut to pieces at Pyongyang on 23 August."
  },
  {
   "id": "yi-sun-sin",
   "title": "Yi Sun-sin's fleet",
   "start": "1592-06-14",
   "end": "1592-10-06",
   "bbox": [
    126.6,
    34.3,
    129.7,
    35.6
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Yi Sun-sin's fleet sails east from Yeosu (June 1592)",
    "Yi Sun-sin lures the Japanese fleet out to Hansan Island (August 1592)",
    "The Korean fleet attacks Busan harbour (October 1592)"
   ],
   "text": "At sea the war went the other way. Admiral Yi Sun-sin of Jeolla sailed east from Yeosu and on 16 June 1592 destroyed a Japanese transport fleet at Okpo. On 15 August he lured Wakisaka Yasuharu's fleet out into open water off Hansan Island and destroyed most of it, and in October he attacked the Japanese fleet in Busan harbour itself. The Japanese could no longer ship supplies along the west coast to their armies in the north."
  },
  {
   "id": "jinju-1592",
   "title": "Jinju and the Righteous Armies",
   "start": "1592-09-05",
   "end": "1592-11-30",
   "bbox": [
    126.2,
    34.5,
    129.6,
    37.2
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "The Japanese march on Jinju (November 1592)"
   ],
   "text": "Behind the Japanese lines, volunteer Righteous Armies led by scholars, officials and Buddhist monks ambushed supply columns and retook towns. Jeolla province, which fed the Korean war effort, was still free, and the fortress of Jinju guarded the road into it. About 30,000 Japanese troops besieged Jinju in November 1592, but Kim Si-min's garrison of under 4,000, with newly made arquebuses, held out for six days until the attackers gave up on 13 November."
  },
  {
   "id": "ming-relief",
   "title": "The Ming army retakes the north",
   "start": "1593-01-15",
   "end": "1593-06-30",
   "bbox": [
    124.3,
    34.8,
    130.2,
    41.5
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Li Rusong's Ming army marches on Pyongyang (January–February 1593)",
    "Li Rusong advances towards Hanseong (February 1593)",
    "The Japanese attack Haengju (March 1593)",
    "The Japanese fall back to the south coast (spring 1593)"
   ],
   "text": "In January 1593 a Ming army of about 40,000 under Li Rusong crossed the Yalu, and with Korean troops it stormed Pyongyang on 8 February. Konishi fell back to Hanseong, and Katō abandoned Hamgyong. The Japanese beat Li's advance guard at Byeokjegwan on 27 February but failed to take Kwon Yul's fort at Haengju on 14 March. Short of food after Korean raids and the burning of their grain stores, they agreed to leave Hanseong and in the spring withdrew to forts on the south coast."
  },
  {
   "id": "jinju-1593",
   "title": "The fall of Jinju and the truce",
   "start": "1593-07-01",
   "end": "1597-03-01",
   "bbox": [
    127.4,
    34.6,
    129.7,
    36.0
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "The Japanese army marches on Jinju (July 1593)"
   ],
   "text": "Hideyoshi demanded revenge for Jinju. In July 1593 about 90,000 Japanese troops besieged the fortress again, broke in on 27 July and killed its defenders and much of its population. Then they withdrew to their wajō, the castles along the Gyeongsang coast from Ulsan to Geoje. For three years Ming and Japanese envoys negotiated, each misleading their own masters, until the talks collapsed at Osaka in 1596 and Hideyoshi ordered a new invasion."
  },
  {
   "id": "second-invasion",
   "title": "The second invasion",
   "start": "1597-08-15",
   "end": "1597-11-30",
   "bbox": [
    125.9,
    34.2,
    130.0,
    37.3
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "The Japanese Left Army marches on Namwon (September 1597)",
    "The Japanese Right Army marches north to Jiksan (September–October 1597)",
    "The Japanese fleet sails west to Myeongnyang (October 1597)"
   ],
   "text": "In 1597 about 140,000 Japanese troops returned. On 28 August they destroyed the Korean fleet at Chilcheollyang, and their Left Army stormed Namwon on 26 September and overran Jeolla while the Right Army marched north. On 16 October a Ming force halted the Japanese at Jiksan, south of Hanseong, and ten days later Yi Sun-sin, restored to command with only 13 ships, beat back a Japanese fleet at Myeongnyang. With winter coming the Japanese fell back to the south coast."
  },
  {
   "id": "last-year",
   "title": "Ulsan, Suncheon and Noryang",
   "start": "1597-12-01",
   "end": "1598-12-22",
   "bbox": [
    126.8,
    34.4,
    129.8,
    36.0
   ],
   "arrows": [
    "Ming–Korean armies attack Ulsan (January 1598)",
    "Allied armies attack Suncheon (September 1598)",
    "Shimazu's relief fleet sails into the Noryang strait (December 1598)"
   ],
   "text": "The Japanese held a chain of castles from Ulsan to Suncheon. A Ming–Korean army failed to take Ulsan in January and February 1598, and allied attacks on Sacheon and Suncheon in the autumn were also beaten off. But Hideyoshi died on 18 September, and Japan's new rulers ordered a withdrawal. On 16 December Yi Sun-sin and the Ming admiral Chen Lin caught Shimazu Yoshihiro's fleet in the Noryang strait; Yi was killed in the battle, and on 21 December the last Japanese sailed from Busan."
  }
